---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'How to Test Your 1RM Safely | 1rmaxcalculator.com';
const description = 'A step-by-step protocol for directly testing your one rep max: warm-up scheme, attempt selection, rest periods, spotter setup, and when not to test.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>How to Test Your 1RM Safely</h1>
  <p>Direct 1RM testing is rarely necessary for everyday training, but it has a place if you're competing or want a precise baseline. Here's how to do it without injuring yourself.</p>

  <h2>Before you start: should you test?</h2>
  <p>Test if: you have at least 6 months of consistent training with the lift, you're injury-free, you have a competent spotter or safety equipment, and you have a specific reason (competition, baseline for a peaking block, etc.).</p>
  <p>Don't test if: you're new to the lift, you're tired or hungover, you have an active injury, or you don't have proper safety equipment (a squat rack with safeties, a bench with spotter arms, or a knowledgeable spotter).</p>

  <h2>Warm-up scheme</h2>
  <p>A common approach for testing a 1RM:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>5 reps with the empty bar</li>
    <li>5 reps at ~40% of your estimated 1RM</li>
    <li>3 reps at ~60%</li>
    <li>2 reps at ~70%</li>
    <li>1 rep at ~80%</li>
    <li>1 rep at ~90%</li>
    <li>Attempt 1: a weight you're certain you can hit (around 95%)</li>
    <li>Attempt 2: a small PR (2&ndash;5 lb / 1&ndash;2.5 kg over your previous 1RM)</li>
    <li>Attempt 3 (optional): if attempt 2 felt easy, another small jump.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Rest 3&ndash;5 minutes between heavy attempts. Don't rush.</p>

  <h2>Attempt selection</h2>
  <p>Pick attempts conservatively. A successful 1RM that's only slightly above your previous best is far more valuable than a failed attempt at an aspirational number. Three successful attempts &gt; one failure.</p>

  <h2>Safety equipment</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Squat:</strong> use a power rack with safety bars set just below your bottom position. A spotter is helpful for bar removal but not strictly required if safeties are set correctly.</li>
    <li><strong>Bench press:</strong> use a rack with spotter arms set just above chest level, OR a competent spotter. Don't bench heavy alone without one or the other.</li>
    <li><strong>Deadlift:</strong> no special equipment needed; you can drop the bar safely. But warm up thoroughly &mdash; cold-start deadlift 1RMs are an injury magnet.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to stop</h2>
  <p>Stop testing if: bar speed drops dramatically on a sub-maximal attempt, your technique breaks down, you feel anything sharp or unusual, or you fail an attempt. A failed attempt usually means you're done for the day &mdash; don't grind out an even heavier one.</p>

  <h2>After testing</h2>
  <p>Take 2&ndash;4 lighter training days after a 1RM test. The recovery cost is real even if you don't feel it the next day. Resume normal programming once bar speed and energy levels return to baseline.</p>

  <p>For programming around your tested 1RM, see our <a href="/guides/percentage-based-programming">percentage-based programming guide</a>.</p>
</BaseLayout>
